### Build a serious search benchmark

This is just as important as writing code.

Amazon's 2026 PROBES research makes a useful distinction between precision errors—bad products surfaced—and **missed recall**, where a genuinely relevant product existed but retrieval failed to surface it. citeturn14search1

Your Noise example is exactly this diagnostic pattern:

```text
Product exists
        ↓
Provider/search candidate set misses it
        ↓
Wrong candidates returned
        ↓
MISSED RECALL

Then wrong candidate reaches UI
        ↓
PRECISION FAILURE
```

Create a frozen human-labelled benchmark for all eight categories covering:

| Test family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Perfect spelling | `Noise Airwave Max 6` |
| Missing character | `airwav max 6` |
| Extra character | `airwaave max 6` |
| Transposition | `ariwave max 6` |
| Keyboard typo | adjacent-key mistakes |
| Phonetic | `paniir`, `chikan` style |
| Joined words | `airwavemax` |
| Split words | `air wave max` |
| Alias | `coke` vs Coca-Cola |
| Hinglish | mixed-language queries |
| Transliteration | regional-script/Latin variants |
| Voice/ASR | speech transcription errors |
| Broad | `headphones` |
| Brand | `Noise headphones` |
| Feature | `ANC headphones` |
| Exact | brand + model |
| Numerical constraint | `128GB`, `650mg` |
| Natural language | `best ANC headphones under 5k` |
| No-match | nonexistent model |
| Hard negative | almost-identical wrong variant |

Google research on voice search also demonstrates that ASR errors can materially degrade search relevance and that query-text post-correction can improve the resulting search experience, so voice-transcription errors deserve their own benchmark family. citeturn15search17

