Amazon uses nDCG in its public ESCI ranking challenge and explicitly evaluates Exact/Substitute/Complement/Irrelevant classification. citeturn14search14

### I would set hard production gates

These would be my proposed TrueCost quality rules:

```text
FALSE_EXACT_MERGES = 0

WRONG_MODEL_IN_EXACT_COMPARISON = 0

WRONG_DOSAGE_IN_EXACT_COMPARISON = 0

WRONG_STORAGE_VARIANT_IN_EXACT_COMPARISON = 0

WRONG_ROUTE_IN_EXACT_COMPARISON = 0

WRONG_FLIGHT_CONTEXT_IN_EXACT_COMPARISON = 0

IRRELEVANT_PRODUCT_AS_AUTHORITATIVE_RESULT = 0

UNKNOWN_ATTRIBUTE_FABRICATED = 0
```

Then aspirational search targets for a large frozen benchmark could begin around:

```text
Exact-query Recall@10       ≥ 98%
Exact-query MRR             ≥ 0.95
Common-typo recovery        ≥ 95%
False autocorrection        < 0.5%
Broad-query NDCG@10         ≥ 0.90
```

Those are **proposed engineering targets**, not claims about current TrueCost performance. They should be adjusted once the first proper benchmark gives us a realistic baseline.

Most importantly:

```text
FALSE_EXACT_MERGES = 0
```

remains a hard invariant even if relaxing it could improve recall.

